# Installation {#sec-install}

Tarn ships two ways: a Python **library** you import, and a standalone **command-line
tool** for one-off queries against a file. Most projects want the library; reach for the
CLI when you just need to slice a CSV from a shell script. Both read the same query
language, so what you learn in one carries over to the other.

## The library {#sec-install-library}

Install into whichever environment manager your project already uses. Tarn has no
required dependencies beyond Arrow, which the wheels bundle.

### pip

```bash
pip install tarn
```

### conda

```bash
conda install -c conda-forge tarn
```

### uv

```bash
uv add tarn
```

Verify the install from a Python prompt:

```python
import tarn
print(tarn.__version__)
```

## The command-line tool {#sec-install-cli}

The `tarn` binary is self-contained: it bundles its own Python runtime, so it does not
touch your project environment.

### macOS

```bash
brew install tarn
```

### Linux

```bash
curl -LsSf https://tarn.dev/install.sh | sh
```

### Windows

```bash
scoop install tarn
```

Confirm the CLI is on your path:

```bash
tarn --version
```

::: {.callout-note}
Tarn needs Python 3.10 or newer for the library. The CLI has no such requirement, since
it carries its own runtime.
:::
